<% content_for :title, "Privacy Policy - Uncal" %>

<div class="bg-white shadow-sm ring-1 ring-gray-200 sm:rounded-lg">
  <div class="px-6 py-8 sm:px-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500">Last updated: May 4, 2026</p>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-sm leading-6 text-gray-700">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Overview</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Uncal is a calendar booking application used to create scheduling links, check calendar availability,
          and manage bookings. This policy explains what information Uncal processes and how it is used.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Information We Process</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Uncal may process account profile information such as names, email addresses, timezone settings,
          team membership, scheduling link settings, booking details, invitee names and email addresses,
          contact notes, workflow email content, and audit or operational logs needed to run the service.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Google Calendar Data</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          When a user connects Google Calendar, Uncal uses Google OAuth tokens to read calendar busy times,
          show availability, create booking events on the user's primary calendar, create Google Meet links
          when requested, and remove calendar events when bookings are cancelled. Uncal does not sell Google
          user data or use it for advertising.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Uncal stores Google OAuth tokens in encrypted form so it can continue syncing availability and
          bookings until the user disconnects the calendar or access is revoked in Google.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">How Information Is Used</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Information is used to authenticate users, operate scheduling links, calculate availability,
          create and cancel bookings, send booking-related emails, support team administration, maintain
          security, troubleshoot issues, and improve reliability.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Sharing</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Booking details may be shared with meeting hosts, invitees, connected calendar providers, and
          email delivery providers as needed to operate the service. Uncal does not sell personal information.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Data Retention And Choices</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Users can disconnect Google Calendar from settings. Administrators can remove users and manage
          team data. Booking and contact data is retained while needed to provide the service, comply with
          operational needs, or until deleted by an authorized user.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Security</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Uncal uses reasonable technical and organizational safeguards, including encrypted storage for
          Google Calendar tokens. No system can be guaranteed completely secure, but access to calendar data
          is limited to the functions needed to provide scheduling.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900">Contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          For privacy questions, contact the administrator of the Uncal deployment you use.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
